( fixup.ff — self-patching first-call resolution for libc symbols )
( fixup — self-patching libc resolution for x86-64. )
( DG's mechanism; Lavarenne's i386 used libc. with direct dlsym. )
(
  i386 fixup replaces a 5-byte "call _xxx" with "mov ebx,funh" [BB imm32].
  On x86-64, function handles are 64-bit, so we can't fit in 5 bytes.
  Instead, fixup writes an 11-byte trampoline to a pre-allocated buffer:
    movabs rbx, funh   [48 BB <8 bytes>]  = 10 bytes
    ret                 [C3]              = 1 byte
  Then patches the 5-byte call's relative offset to point to the trampoline.
  On subsequent calls, the patched call goes directly to the trampoline,
  which loads the function handle and returns — no resolution overhead.

  IMPORTANT: Trampolines are NOT written at here [rbp] because during
  anonymous block execution, here points to the executing code itself
  [_semi_exec resets rbp]. Writing there would overwrite running code.
  Instead, _fixbuf provides dedicated executable memory for trampolines.

  x86-64 difference from i386: fixup relies on tail-call optimization
  [E8->E9] so it is JMP'd to, not called — no extra return address on R.
  i386 has rdrop because fixup there is called [E8], not jumped to.

  IMPORTANT: The hidden definition MUST end with explicit semicolon:
    :. _xxx "symbol" fixup ;
  Without the ;, _colon does not terminate the previous named def, so
  tail-call optimization never triggers and fixup patches the wrong call.
  In the REPL, _auto implicitly adds ; at end of line, masking this.
  In loadfile [-f], no _auto runs — the ; must be explicit.
)
variable _fixbuf pvt 1024 allot
variable _fixptr pvt
_fixbuf _fixptr! ;
: fixup ( addr len -- )
  libc@ #fun
  _fixptr@
  $48 over c! $BB over 1+ c!
  2dup 2+ !
  $C3 over 10+ c!
  dup 11+ _fixptr!
  nip
  r> 5- dup>r
  - 5- r 1+ d!
;
" fixup" features append ;
